course: everyday
lesson: 466
title: Joining a game, and the shy kid
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Lily is shy and wants to join the skipping game. Three breaks in a row she stands near the rope, pretending to tie her shoelace, until the lace is nearly worn through. On the fourth day Maddie says, «Wanna turn? Ellie's arms are dead.» Lily turns, then jumps, then sings the rhyme louder than anyone. She's in. That's how joining usually works: stand close, watch, offer to help — turn the rope, fetch the ball — and then ask.
explain: Sam asks some boys, «Can I play?» «Nah, teams are even, mate.» He goes red and walks off fast. Ten minutes later two boys go in, one with a nosebleed, one hunting a lost jumper. Sam asks again: «Go on then, you're with us.» He scores. Connor tries the opposite way: he barges into a game he isn't in, grabs the ball and shouts, «Oi, YOU, mark him, you're rubbish!» «Who even are you?» they say, and tell him to get lost. Next day he asks politely and plays where they put him. By Friday he's picked first.
explain: Jake is too cool to say he wants to play conkers. «Conkers is so mid, it's literally a nut on a string,» he says — and watches every single game. When Sam says «Want a go, then?», Jake shrugs «whatever» and plays for an hour; his conker's a sixer by the bell. At his cousin's birthday Kieran knows nobody and hides in the kitchen with the dog, until his auntie hands him a bowl: «Be a love, go round with the crisps.» Suddenly everyone talks to him, mostly about crisps. By the end he's in the water fight. In Swansea Lily's cousin tells the local kids, «This is Lily, be nice, she's lush» — Welsh for lovely. Lily has never played rounders; she misses five times, then whacks the ball into the duck pond, and everyone, Lily too, screams laughing. She's one of them now. And when Ruby beats Sam at chess in nine moves and says «GG, easy» — good game — Sam laughs and asks for a rematch next week.
explain: Shy people need the right kind of push. Hana reads thick fantasy books alone every break. Chloe sits on the next bench with her own book and says nothing. After a week Hana leans over: «This bit. The dragon eats the king's crown. I died.» Mia does it wrong: «Why are you so quiet? Can you even talk?» in front of the whole table, and the new boy leaves red-faced. Two days later she asks him, «Do you like drawing?» — he shows her a notebook of amazing robots. New boy Theo has a stammer; kids keep finishing his words for him — «b-b-bus? Bus. He means bus» — until he stops putting his hand up. The teacher asks the class to wait and let him finish. By half-term Theo tells the whole class a joke, start to finish, and it's a good one.
fact: To join a game, stand close, watch, offer to help, then ask to play.
fact: A «no» to joining a game isn't always forever; asking again when the game changes often works.
fact: Barging in and bossing people about makes others want you out; fitting in first works better.
fact: Pretending you don't care about a game makes joining harder; just say «Can I have a go?»
fact: Having a small job, like handing round food at a party, helps shy people start talking.
fact: Some shy people want quiet company first; sitting nearby and letting them come to you can work.
fact: Pointing out someone's shyness in front of others embarrasses them; asking about something they like works better.
fact: A stammer means a person's words sometimes repeat or get stuck; it has nothing to do with how clever they are.
fact: When someone stammers, wait, look at them and let them finish their own words.
fact: Being bad at a game can still help you make friends if you laugh at yourself.
fact: Being beaten by a friend can be fun, and asking people to play is how many friendships start.
fact: In Wales «lush» means lovely; «GG» means «good game»; in conkers a «sixer» is a conker that has beaten six others.
exercise: How did Lily finally get into the skipping game? | She offered to turn the rope when Ellie's arms were tired | She cried loudly by the wall until they felt sorry and let her in | She grabbed the rope and ran | She asked the teacher to order them
exercise: What is a good way to join a game? | Watch, help, then ask to play | Grab the ball and start shouting | Wait for a formal invitation card | Ask a teacher to make them let you in
exercise: Sam was told «teams are even». What worked later? | Asking again when two boys went in | Taking their ball home until they said yes | Crying until they gave in | Making his own team of one
exercise: Why did the boys tell Connor to get lost? | He barged in and bossed them about | He was too small to play | He was wearing the wrong colour for their team | He was too good at football
exercise: What did Connor do next day that worked? | Asked politely and played where they put him | Brought a better ball and said he'd share it with them | Barged in louder | Paid them to let him in
exercise: Why did Jake say conkers is «mid»? | He wanted to play but acted too cool | He really hated conkers and always had | He was allergic to conkers | He had never seen conkers
exercise: What happened when Sam asked Jake, «Want a go, then?» | He played for an hour | He walked away to the bench | He stamped on the conkers | He told a teacher on Sam
exercise: How did Kieran stop hiding at the party? | His auntie gave him crisps to hand round | The dog took him outside to play in the garden | He fell asleep in the kitchen | He went home early
exercise: Why did handing round crisps help? | It gave him a job and a reason to talk | Crisps make everyone friendly, whoever hands them out | Nobody else could carry a bowl | He ate them all himself
exercise: How did Chloe make friends with quiet Hana? | Sat nearby reading until Hana came to her | Took Hana's book away | Asked her loudly in class why she's always so quiet | Made her play football
exercise: Mia asked «Can you even talk?» in front of everyone. What went wrong? | It made the shy boy the joke | He couldn't hear her over the noise | It was much too quiet for anyone to hear | Nothing, it was fine to ask
exercise: What worked better for Mia two days later? | Asking about something he likes | Asking even louder | Ignoring him for good | Telling the whole table that he's very shy
exercise: What does a stammer mean? | Words sometimes repeat or get stuck | The person isn't clever | The person is lying | The person is only nervous of you and nobody else
exercise: What should you do when someone stammers? | Wait and let them finish their own words | Finish their words for them quickly, to save time | Laugh to make them relax | Look away and talk to someone else
exercise: Lily hit the rounders ball into the duck pond. Why did it help her make friends? | Everyone, Lily too, laughed together | They all felt sorry for her and said so | The ducks liked her | She bought them new balls
exercise: «GG, easy,» says Ruby after beating Sam in nine moves. What does «GG» mean? | Good game | Go, go, go | Get gone, loser | Giant grandmaster
exercise: «Be nice, she's lush.» What does «lush» mean in Wales? | Lovely | Wet through | Very loud | Very shy
test: «nah teams are even, jog on». What's the best next move? | Watch, then ask again when the game changes | Never ask anyone ever again | Take the ball off them | Call them names from far away, then run off
test: Connor: «I'm the best player here, OI, pass it, you lot are trash». What would most likely happen? | They'd want him out of the game | They'd make him captain | They'd pass to him every time | They'd thank him for the tips
test: Theo says «c-c-can I—». Someone shouts «CAN HE GO TOILET, MISS». What should happen instead? | Wait and let Theo finish | Finish faster for him | Laugh so he relaxes | Talk over him to help
test: «stammering means you're a bit slow, innit». Is that true? | No, it has nothing to do with being clever | Yes, everyone knows that it means you're slow | Yes, but only for boys | No, it means you're lying
test: In a cartoon, the shy kid gets dragged on stage and becomes a star in one minute. What helps shy kids more in real life? | Quiet company and a small, friendly step | Being shoved into the spotlight as soon as possible | Being told «just stop being shy» | Being laughed at until they speak
test: Kieran's at a party where he knows no one. Which is the best move? | Offer to help, like handing round crisps | Hide with the dog in the garden all afternoon | Leave without saying anything | Tell everyone he's bored
test: Jake: «conkers is for babies». He's been watching for 20 mins. What's he really saying? | He wants to play but won't admit it | He hates conkers | He's guarding the conkers for his mates | He's waiting for his mum to come
test: Lily misses the ball five times at rounders. Her cousin's mates are laughing. What's the best way to handle it? | Laugh at herself and keep playing | Storm off and go home | Hit someone with the bat | Say rounders is stupid
test: What would most likely have happened if Mia had kept asking «can you even talk?» every day? | The boy would have hidden from her more | He'd have become her best friend by the end of the week | He'd have started shouting | Nothing would change
test: Ruby beats Sam at chess in nine moves and says «GG, easy». What's a good reply? | «Rematch next week?» | «You cheated, I'm telling» | Throw the pieces on the floor | Never play chess again
test: Chloe sits next to Hana for a week without talking. Her mate says «that's just weird». Was it a good idea? | Yes, quiet company let Hana come to her | No, she should have grabbed Hana's book | No, you must talk on day one | Yes, but only because it was raining
exam: Identify the procedure most likely to secure admission to a game already in progress. | Observing, assisting, and thereafter requesting to participate | Seizing the ball and issuing instructions to the existing players | Awaiting a formal written invitation | Demanding that a teacher compel admission
exam: Given that a pupil speaks with a stammer, ascertain the most appropriate conduct of his classmates. | Waiting patiently for him to complete his own words | Completing each of his words on his behalf | Laughing in order to put him at ease | Addressing their remarks to other persons instead
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the effect of remarking publicly upon a pupil's quietness? | It tends to embarrass the pupil and to increase his withdrawal | It invariably encourages the pupil to speak | It is the recommended method of befriending a shy pupil | It has no effect whatsoever upon the pupil
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a shy child at a social gathering. | The assignment of a small task may assist the child in conversing | The child should be left undisturbed in isolation throughout the gathering | The child should be compelled to perform before the guests | The child should be informed that shyness is a fault
